{{{
- The legend of Count Dracula finds its origins in the historical figure Vlad III, also known as Vlad the Impaler or Vlad Dracula, a 15th-century prince of Wallachia, a region in present-day Romania. Vlad III was known for his brutal methods of punishing his enemies, often using impalement, which contributed to his fearsome reputation. His moniker "Dracula" is derived from the Romanian word "Drăculea," meaning "son of Dracul," a reference to his father, Vlad II, who was a member of the Order of the Dragon—a chivalric order founded to defend Christianity in Eastern Europe. Over time, tales of Vlad's cruelty and his association with the dragon led to the conflation of his historical persona with mythical elements, laying the groundwork for the legend that would inspire the fictional character of Count Dracula.
}}}

{{{
- The most famous literary adaptation of the Dracula legend is undoubtedly Bram Stoker's 1897 novel, "Dracula." Stoker's work took the historical elements of Vlad Dracula and transformed them into a gothic horror masterpiece, introducing Count Dracula as a Transylvanian vampire who travels to England to spread the undead curse. The novel draws on various mythologies and folklore, weaving together the fear of the unknown and the allure of immortality. Stoker's Dracula is characterized by his supernatural powers, including shapeshifting, commanding nocturnal creatures, and seductive charm, all of which cemented the vampire as a staple of horror literature. The novel's epistolary format, combining letters, diary entries, and newspaper clippings, created a sense of realism that captivated readers and set the standard for subsequent vampire fiction.
}}}

{{{
- The legend of Count Dracula has significantly impacted Transylvanian culture and tourism, transforming the region into a popular destination for fans of gothic literature and vampire lore. Bran Castle, often referred to as "Dracula's Castle," is one of Romania's top tourist attractions, although its association with Vlad the Impaler is tenuous. The castle's dramatic architecture and picturesque setting in the Carpathian Mountains enhance its allure as a symbol of Dracula's haunting presence. The town of Sighisoara, Vlad's birthplace, and other sites linked to his life and legend, attract visitors interested in both the historical and fictional aspects of Dracula. This fascination has fostered a vibrant tourism industry, with themed tours, festivals, and events celebrating the Dracula mythos, contributing to the local economy and cultural identity of Transylvania.
}}}

